tation in older adults (57yo) is more typical of CML in blast crisis (B1.2) than rapidly progressive de novo AML (B1.1), which often presents acutely in younger patients."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the subacute onset over days argue against de novo AML (B1.1) in favor of a chronic myeloproliferative process in transformation (e.g., CML in blast crisis, B1.2)?", "summary": "Subacute progression over days reduces the likelihood of de novo AML (B1.1), which typically presents with abrupt symptom onset, while favoring chronic processes undergoing transformation."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of prior CML documentation and presence of constitutional symptoms (fever, weight loss) undermine the plausibility of CML in blast crisis (B1.2) compared to de novo AML (B1.1)?", "summary": "Lack of documented CML history and prominent constitutional symptoms strongly argue against CML in blast crisis (B1.2), as blast crisis typically occurs in patients with known chronic phase CML."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the subacute progression over days and older adult age (57yo) argue in favor of CML in blast crisis (B1.2) compared to de novo AML (B1.1), given the typical clinical course of blast crisis?", "summary": "Subacute course in older adults is more typical of CML in blast crisis, but the lack of CML history weakens this argument."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the lack of documented CML history and presence of constitutional symptoms (fever, weight loss) undermine the plausibility of CML in blast crisis (B1.2) in favor of de novo AML (B1.1)?", "summary": "Absence of CML history and presence of systemic symptoms strongly reduces likelihood of blast crisis while supporting a de novo acute leukemia diagnosis."}, {"t": 4, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of 35% blasts in peripheral blood with constitutional symptoms (fever, weight loss) and systemic involvement (neurologic ataxia) strongly support de novo acute myeloid leukemia (AML…", "summary": "The presence of 35% blasts in peripheral blood with constitutional symptoms (fever, weight loss) and systemic involvement (neurologic ataxia) provides weak support for de novo acute myeloid leukemia (AML) while minimally reducing the likelihood of myelodysplastic syndrome with excess blasts."}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No universal instability criteria are met (airway intact, respiratory function preserved, no hypotension/tachycardia with end-organ signs, no severe altered mental status, no uncontrolled hemorrhage). Time-critical conditions (STEMI, stroke, septic shock, tension pneumothorax) are not indicated by the clinical picture.", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading terminal branch B1.1 (AML) remains below 0.75 posterior threshold. Dangerous alternatives (B1.1, B1.2) with posteriors ≥0.10 persist. Max tree depth (3) not yet reached, and untried discriminators (BCR-ABL1 testing, bone marrow biopsy) retain diagnostic value."}, "turn_budget_used": 4, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.3, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.4159611608780137, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Acute lymphoblastic leukemia"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Acute myelogenous leukemia"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Chronic myelogenous leukemia"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Multiple myeloma"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Acute myeloid leukemia', 'constitutional symptom', 'elevated white blood cell count', 'elevated red blood cell count', 'fever', 'Multiple myeloma', 'weight loss'}", "mode_policy": {"bench